@ 17.7.2.5 - West Germanic to Old Low German
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Old Saxon phonology”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. Umlaut applies; going by the orthography, only a → e / _(C…){i(ː),j} is often marked (and even then haphazardly), but based upon reflexes in the daughter languages it seems that the umlaut had to apply to the other back vowels too
	{ ai, au } > { e:[+long], o:[+long] }
	β > v
	v:[+long] ɣ:[+long] h:[+long] > b:[+long] ɡ:[+long] x:[+long] (perhaps not strictly a sound change, but worth noting)
	{ f, θ, s } > { v, ð, z } / syllable-initially
	v > f / _C // _d
	{ b, d } > { p, t } / _C:[-voice]
	k > t͡s / _:[+front] (ɡ → d͡z here?)
	n > ŋ / :{ _k, _ɡ }:
	ɡ > k / ŋ_#
	ɡ > ʝ / _:[+front] (singleton only)
	ɡ > ɣ / _V (singleton only)
	ɡ > x / _#
	F:[-voice] > F:[+voice] / X:[+voice]_X:[+voice]?